.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt QUARK_QUEUE_INJECT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm quark_queue_inject
.Nd inject a decoded synthetic event
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In quark.h
.Ft int
.Fn quark_queue_inject "struct quark_queue *qq" "const struct quark_synthetic_event *event"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
copies and inserts the decoded synthetic event pointed to by
.Fa event
into the raw-event trees of
.Fa qq .
The queue must have been opened with the
.Dv QQ_SYNTHETIC
backend.
.Pp
The synthetic backend and this function are omitted from normal builds.
Build with
.Ev WITH_SYNTHETIC=1
to enable them.
.Pp
This interface is benchmark and test support.
It enters the production event path immediately before internal
.Fn raw_event_insert
and therefore exercises raw-event sorting and aggregation, process-cache
updates and garbage collection, userspace rules, and
.Vt quark_event
construction.
It does not execute probes, transport events through ring buffers, decode
backend records, or evaluate BPF-side filters.
.Pp
Supported
.Va event.kind
values are:
.Bl -tag -width QUARK_SYNTHETIC_FILE_MODIFY
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_FORK
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_EXEC
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_EXIT
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_SETSID
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_FILE_CREATE
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_FILE_MODIFY
.It Dv QUARK_SYNTHETIC_FILE_DELETE
.El
.Pp
.Va event.time
is a required nonzero timestamp in nanoseconds since
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME .
.Va event.process.pid
is required for every event.
A fork, exec, exit or setsid additionally requires a non-empty
.Va event.process.comm ,
matching the backends, which always report the kernel's
.Va task->comm .
An exec additionally requires a non-empty
.Va event.process.executable ;
a file event requires a non-empty
.Va event.file.path .
.Pp
Process input includes credentials, capabilities, parent, process-group and
session relationships, controlling TTY, namespaces, start and exit data,
cwd, cgroup, argv, environment, comm, and executable.
It applies to fork, exec, exit and setsid events only.
.Va argv
and
.Va executable
are recorded by an exec only, which is the only event a backend reports them
on.
Both are ignored on a fork, exit or setsid, so a caller may reuse one process
for a whole lifecycle.
A fork still reports a cmdline, inherited from its parent as it is with a
backend.
A file event uses just
.Va event.process.pid
and the file fields; its process is resolved through the process cache, so it
reports no process until an earlier event has populated the cache for that
pid.
This matches the backends, whose file events also carry only a pid.
File input includes paths, inode, timestamps, size, mode, ownership and change
mask.
.Va argv
and
.Va env
are NUL-delimited buffers whose final byte must be NUL.
Environment input is limited by the queue's
.Va max_env
attribute in the same way as decoded backend input.
.Pp
All strings and buffers are copied before
.Nm
returns, so the caller retains ownership and may immediately reuse them.
Injection is synchronous.
Like the rest of a
.Vt quark_queue ,
it is not safe to call concurrently on the same queue.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Zero on success, -1 otherwise and
.Va errno
is set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The queue is not synthetic, a pointer is NULL, the event kind is unsupported,
or a required field or NUL-delimited buffer is malformed.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Memory for the copied event could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The combined file-path storage length overflowed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr quark_process_lookup 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_close 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_get_event 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_get_stats 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_open 3 ,
.Xr quark 7
